---
import PrimerChat from "../PrimerChat.astro";
import { aiCardId, aiTurn, archiveAi } from "../../../lib/archive-ai";
import { fridayPages } from "../../../lib/archive";
import { spell } from "../../../lib/cases";

const card = archiveAi["case-fingerprint"];
const saved = spell(fridayPages.length);

type Output = { name: string; code: string; setting?: string; input: string };
const outputs: Output[] = [
  { name: "Monthly totals", code: "totals cell", setting: "timezone", input: "sales.csv" },
  { name: "Sales chart", code: "chart cell", input: "sales.csv" },
  { name: "Inventory report", code: "inventory cell", input: "inventory.csv" },
];
type Change = { id: string; label: string; touches?: string; comment?: boolean; upstream?: boolean };
const changes: Change[] = [
  { id: "tz", label: "Change the timezone setting", touches: "timezone" },
  { id: "sales", label: "Correct sales.csv", touches: "sales.csv" },
  { id: "comment", label: "Edit a comment in the totals cell", touches: "totals cell", comment: true },
  { id: "none", label: "Change nothing" },
  { id: "upstream", label: "Upstream file changes, not fetched", touches: "sales.csv", upstream: true },
];
const verdict = (c: Change, o: Output): [string, string, string] => {
  const recipe = !!c.touches && (c.touches === o.code || c.touches === o.setting);
  const input = !!c.touches && c.touches === o.input;
  if (c.upstream && input) return ["Reuse", "is-warn", "The local copy's hash still matches. The newer upstream file is invisible."];
  if (recipe && c.comment) return ["Rebuild", "is-ok", "The fingerprint differs. The output will come out the same."];
  if (recipe) return ["Rebuild", "is-ok", "The recipe fingerprint differs."];
  if (input) return ["Rebuild", "is-ok", "The input hash differs."];
  return ["Reuse", "is-quiet", "Fingerprint and input hash both match."];
};

const salesFix = changes.find((c) => c.id === "sales")!;
const tryIt = changes.filter((c) => c !== salesFix);

const checks = [
  ["A hidden setting", "A memoized function takes a price and reads the tax rate from a global setting. What should its cache key account for, and why?"],
  ["One hash differs", "A report's recipe fingerprint matches its stored value, but one of its input hashes differs. Should the report be reused? How might a comment-only edit behave in a system that hashes whole source files?"],
  ["Outdated but verified", "A notebook verifies the hash of a downloaded CSV every time it runs. How could it still show outdated data, and what additional check would catch that?"],
];
---

<PrimerChat
  prompt={card.prompt}
  module="Module 2"
  back={`../02-archive/#${aiCardId("case-fingerprint")}`}
  about="This is a reply an AI assistant gave to Module 2's stale-output study prompt, lightly edited. Send the same prompt yourself and you will get a different reply."
  turn={aiTurn("case-fingerprint", "Case 3")}
>
  <h2 class="pr-title">How a cache decides what is stale</h2>
  <p class="pr-pull">A cached output is a result plus a record of how it was produced.</p>
  <p>To decide whether to reuse it, compare that record with what the step would use if it ran now.</p>

  <h2>1. Fingerprint the recipe and its settings</h2>
  <p>A fingerprint is a compact identifier, usually a hash, computed from the code and configuration that determine what a step does. Suppose a notebook cell reads sales data and computes monthly totals. If the cell reads a <code>timezone</code> setting, changing that setting can move a sale from one month to another. The cell's code can stay byte-for-byte identical while its correct output changes. The timezone therefore belongs in the fingerprint.</p>
  <p>Memoization follows the same rule. A cache for <code>monthly_totals(data, timezone)</code> can key on both arguments. If the function instead reads the timezone from a global setting, the cache still needs to account for it.</p>

  <h2>2. Check the recipe and the inputs</h2>
  <p>When the step runs, store its fingerprint and the hash of each input it actually used. On a later run, mark the output stale if <strong>either</strong> comparison differs.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Same sales file, different timezone → the recipe fingerprint changed.</li>
    <li>Same code and timezone, corrected sales file → the input hash changed.</li>
    <li>Both match → the cached output can be reused, assuming the record covers everything that affects the result.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>This lets a build tool make the decision for each output from its recorded dependencies. If two reports read the sales file and a third reads only inventory, a sales correction invalidates the two sales reports.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card">
    <p class="pr-label">A corrected sales.csv, checked output by output</p>
    <div class="pr-scroll">
      <table class="pr-table pa-fp-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Output</th><th scope="col">Reads</th><th scope="col">Recipe fingerprint</th><th scope="col">Input hash</th><th scope="col">Verdict</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{outputs.map((o) => {
          const [v, tone] = verdict(salesFix, o);
          return (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{o.name}</th>
              <td><code>{o.input}</code></td>
              <td>Same</td>
              <td class:list={{ "is-diff": o.input === salesFix.touches }}>{o.input === salesFix.touches ? "Differs" : "Same"}</td>
              <td><span class:list={["pr-chip", tone]}>{v}</span></td>
            </tr>
          );
        })}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <figcaption>Each output compares its own record. No one has to decide which reports the correction touched.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p><code>make</code> illustrates the dependency rule. By default, it compares file modification times. If a declared prerequisite is newer than its target, <code>make</code> rebuilds the target. A configuration file can be declared as a prerequisite so its edits trigger the same check. Content-based build tools use hashes to make a similar decision.</p>

  <h2>3. Rebuilding too much versus too little</h2>
  <p>For a repeatable build step, an unnecessary rebuild spends time and compute to produce a result you could have reused. A missed rebuild can leave an old report in place and let the run finish successfully. Anyone reading the report may assume it reflects the latest inputs.</p>
  <p>Some systems deliberately hash a whole source file, comments included. Hashing bytes is simple and avoids having to determine which edits affect behavior. A comment edit may cause an unnecessary rebuild, and the system accepts that cost.</p>

  <h2>4. A fingerprint can only describe what the system observed</h2>
  <p>Suppose the notebook hashes a local copy of a CSV downloaded yesterday. The upstream CSV changes today, but the notebook never fetches or checks it again. The local hash still describes yesterday's copy accurately. To detect the upstream change, the workflow needs a fresh fetch or a check against a trustworthy upstream version identifier. Untracked dependencies, such as an environment variable the step reads, create the same kind of blind spot.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card pa-fp">
    <fieldset class="pr-seg">
      <legend>Try it: sections 1 to 4 in one notebook. Make one change</legend>
      {tryIt.map((c, i) => <label><input type="radio" name="pa-fp-change" value={c.id} checked={i === 0} /><span>{c.label}</span></label>)}
    </fieldset>
    <div aria-live="polite">
    {tryIt.map((c) => (
      <div class="pa-fp-view" data-change={c.id}>
      <label class="pa-reveal"><input type="checkbox" /> <span>Predict which outputs rebuild, then show the verdicts</span></label>
      <ul class="pa-fp-out">
        {outputs.map((o) => {
          const [v, tone, why] = verdict(c, o);
          return (
            <li>
              <p class="pa-fp-head"><b>{o.name}</b><span class:list={["pr-chip", "pa-fp-v", tone]}>{v}</span></p>
              <p class="pa-fp-deps">{[o.code, o.setting, o.input].filter(Boolean).join(" · ")}</p>
              <p class="pa-fp-why">{why}</p>
            </li>
          );
        })}
      </ul>
      </div>
    ))}
    </div>
    <figcaption>Each output keeps the fingerprint of its code and settings, and the hash of the file it read. Only a difference in one of those triggers a rebuild.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <aside class="pr-course" aria-label="Course note">
    <p class="pr-label">Course note</p>
    <p>The lab's fingerprint hashes the parser's whole module, the shared page reader it imports, and the config the lab covers with it: the alias list the parser reads, and the membership file and season that pick the standings' teams. That is the reply's whole-source-file choice. One parser reads all {saved} saved schedules, so a comment edit in it makes all {saved} stale, where the figure's comment edit touched only one output. Each parsed schedule also keeps the content hash of the saved page it read, the reply's input hash.</p>
    <p>Case 3's last change is the reply's section 4. Harbor Point's site posted a result after Friday, but the saved page did not change, so its hash still matches and nothing looks stale until something fetches it.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>5. Check your understanding</h2>
  <ol class="pr-checks">{checks.map(([name, q]) => <li><b>{name}</b><span>{q}</span></li>)}</ol>
</PrimerChat>

<style>
  :global(.pr-thread) { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .pa-fp .pr-seg { margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
  .pa-fp-table code { white-space: nowrap; }
  .pa-fp-table td.is-diff { background: color-mix(in srgb, #d9822b 18%, transparent); font-weight: 600; }
  .pa-fp-table :global(.pr-chip.is-quiet) { border: 1px solid var(--tl-line); background: transparent; color: var(--tl-muted); }
  .pa-fp-view { display: none; }
  .pa-fp-view:not(:has(.pa-reveal input:checked)) .pa-fp-v { visibility: hidden; }
  .pa-fp-view:not(:has(.pa-reveal input:checked)) .pa-fp-why { display: none; }
  .pa-reveal { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.45rem; min-height: 40px; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; font-size: 0.88rem; cursor: pointer; }
  .pa-reveal input { width: 1.1rem; height: 1.1rem; accent-color: var(--pr-ai); }
  .pa-fp-out { display: grid; gap: 0.5rem; margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  @media (min-width: 46rem) { .pa-fp-out { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }
  .pa-fp:has([value="tz"]:checked) [data-change="tz"],
  .pa-fp:has([value="comment"]:checked) [data-change="comment"],
  .pa-fp:has([value="none"]:checked) [data-change="none"],
  .pa-fp:has([value="upstream"]:checked) [data-change="upstream"] { display: block; }
  .pa-fp-out li { padding: 0.55rem 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--tl-line); border-radius: 8px; background: var(--tl-bg); font-size: 0.88rem; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-fp-out p { margin: 0 0 0.25rem; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-fp-out p:last-child { margin: 0; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-fp-head { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem; }
  .pa-fp-head b { color: var(--sl-color-white); }
  :global(.pr) .pa-fp-deps { color: var(--tl-muted); font-size: 0.78rem; }
  .pa-fp-out :global(.pr-chip.is-quiet) { border: 1px solid var(--tl-line); background: transparent; color: var(--tl-muted); }
</style>
